    <ferc:ProvisionForDeferredIncomeTaxesCreditOtherIncomeAndDeductions id="f-1426" contextRef="c-30" unitRef="u-3" decimals="0">1706624</ferc:ProvisionForDeferredIncomeTaxesCreditOtherIncomeAndDeductions>
    <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-1428" contextRef="c-30">&lt;div class="Section_1"&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 27.35pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-indent: -27.35pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;LS POWER GRID CALIFORNIA, LLC&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center"&gt; 				&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;NOTES TO FINANCIAL STATEMENTS&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;December 31, 2025 and 2024&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin: 0.05pt 12.95pt 10pt 5.75pt; line-height: 102%"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin: 0.05pt 12.95pt 10pt 5.75pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 102%"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The accompanying financial statements were prepared in accordance with the accounting requirements of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC) as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than U.S. generally accepted accounting principles (U.S. GAAP). Accordingly, certain footnotes are not prepared on the same basis as the financial statements presented herein as the Notes to the Financial Statements set forth below are principally from the Company's audited December 31, 2025 and 2024 U.S. GAAP Financial Statements.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin: 0 12.95pt 0 5.75pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 108%"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The primary differences between the Company's FERC financial statements and U.S. GAAP financial statements are:&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin: 0 12.95pt 0 5.75pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 108%"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 14pt; margin-left: 24pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-indent: -18pt; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 5.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Accumulated costs of removal are reported in accumulated depreciation for FERC reporting purposes (U.S. GAAP requires that cost of removal be classified as a regulatory liability);&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin: 0 12.95pt 0 24pt; text-indent: -18pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 108%"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(2)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 5.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;There is no current liability classification of the current portion of long term debt; and deferred financing costs are reported separately in the FERC balance sheet;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin: 0 12.9pt 0 24pt; text-indent: -18pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 108%"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(3)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 5.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;The presentation of regulatory assets and regulatory liabilities in the balance sheet under U.S. GAAP requirements differs from FERC requirements;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin: 0 12.9pt 0 24pt; text-indent: -18pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 108%"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(4)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 5.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="letter-spacing: -0.45pt"&gt;The inclusion of income taxes as a component of the FERC financial statements pursuant to the FERC's Policy Statement on Income Tax Allowances, rather than the exclusion of income taxes pursuant to U.S. GAAP due to the Company's treatment as a flow through entity; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin: 0 12.8pt 0 24pt; text-indent: -18pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 108%"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(5)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 5.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;A portion of Member's equity for U.S. GAAP reporting purposes is included in Accumulated Deferred Income Taxes for FERC reporting purposes. However, the Members pay income taxes on their allocated share of the Company's income and for purposes herein, those taxes are calculated using the federal corporate rate.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin: 0 12.8pt 0 24pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 108%"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 40.18pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -40.18pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;(1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 27.35pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Organization&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 27.35pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;California Transmission Development, LLC (California Transmission) was formed on August 15, 2008 as a Delaware limited liability company. The name of California Transmission was amended to LS Power Grid California, LLC (the Company) on February 28, 2019. The Company is owned by LS Power Grid California Holdings, LLC (California Holdings). &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 27.35pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company was formed to develop, own, and operate transmission projects in the area operated by the California Independent System Operator Corporation (CAISO).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt; In January 2020, CAISO designated the Company as the approved project sponsor for the Gates Dynamic Reactive Support (DRS) Project (Gates Project) and in February 2020, CAISO designated the Company as the approved project sponsor for the Round Mountain Area DRS Project (RM Project). The Gates Project is a 500 kV dynamic reactive power support substation, to be known as Orchard Substation, which includes an approximately 850 MVAR (megavolt-ampere, reactive) static synchronous compensator (STATCOM) device to be installed in two equally-sized blocks independently connected to the Pacific Gas and Electric Company (PG&amp;amp;E) Gates substation. The RM Project is a 500 kV dynamic reactive power support substation, to be known as Fern Road Substation, which includes an approximately 530 MVAR STATCOM to be installed in two equally-sized blocks independently connected to each of the existing PG&amp;amp;E Round Mountain to Table Mountain 500 kV transmission lines at the new Fern Road Substation located south of PG&amp;amp;E's Round Mountain substation. The Gates Project was placed in service on March 12, 2025.The RM Project is expected to be placed in service in 2026.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 27.35pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 27.35pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In January 2023, the Company was selected by CAISO to construct two new transmission projects, Manning (Manning Project) and Collinsville (Collinsville Project). The Manning Project will be located in Fresno County with a 500/230 kV substation and an approximately 12-mile double circuit 230 kV transmission line.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt; The Collinsville Project will be located in Solano County and Contra Costa County with a 500/230 kV substation and an approximately 6-mile double circuit 230 kV transmission line including a submarine crossing of the Sacramento River.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Each project is expected to be in-service in2028. Additionally, in 2025, CAISO approved a new 500 kV transmission line from Manning to Metcalf (Manning to Metcalf Project). As the incumbent owner of the Manning substation, the Company will be responsible for installing a new 500 kV line terminal position and transmission line segment from the Manning substation to a point near the Company's property boundary. This project is expected to be in service in 2034.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 27.35pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In March 2023, the Company was selected by CAISO to build, own, and operate two new High-Voltage Direct Current (HVDC) transmission projects in the San Jose area (Metcalf-San Jose B (the Metcalf Project) and Newark-Northern Receiving Station (the Newark Project)). In November 2024, CAISO adjusted the scope of these projects to better meet growing demand forecasts. With those adjustments, the Newark Project is now a 230 kV transmission line which is approximately 12 miles long and predominately underground. The Metcalf Project includes two new HVDC terminals and approximately 13 miles of predominantly underground transmission lines. Each project is expected to be in-service in 2028.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 27.35pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12pt; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In June 2024, CAISO approved two new 500 kV transmission lines from New Humboldt to Fern Road and from New Humboldt to Collinsville. As the incumbent transmission owner of the Fern Road and Collinsville substations, the Company will be responsible for installing a new 500 kV line terminal position and transmission line segment from the substations to a point near the Company's property boundary at Fern Road and Collinsville. Each project is expected to be in service in 2034.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 40.18pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -40.18pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;(2)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 27.35pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Summary of Significant Accounting Policies&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(a)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Basis of Presentation&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: unset; font-size: 1em"&gt;The Company qualifies for the application of regulatory accounting treatment as allowed by the Financial Accounting Standards Board (FASB) Accounting Standards Codification (ASC) 980, Regulated Operations (ASC 980). This accounting guidance prescribes the method to be used to record the financial transactions of a regulated entity. The criteria for applying the accounting guidance for regulated operations include the following: (i) rates are set by an independent third party regulator, (ii) approved rates are intended to recover the specific costs of the regulated products or services, and (iii) rates set at levels that will recover costs that can be charged to and collected from customers. The accounting guidance for regulated operations recognizes that the rate actions of a regulator can provide reasonable assurance of the existence of an asset and requires the capitalization of incurred costs that would otherwise be charged to expense where it is probable that future revenue will be provided to recover these costs. Additionally, the rate actions of a regulator can impose a liability on a regulated entity and such liabilities are usually obligations to the entity's customers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;These financial statements and notes reflect the Company's evaluation of events occurring subsequent to the accompanying balance sheet dates through March12, 2026, the date the U.S. GAAP financial statements were issued. This evaluation has been updated through April 15, 2026, the date the Company filed this report with FERC.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(b)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Management makes estimates and assumptions relating to the reporting of assets and liabilities and the disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities at the date of the financial statements and reported amounts of revenues and expenses to prepare the financial statements in conformity with U.S.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;GAAP. The most significant of these estimates and assumptions relate to the valuation of derivative instrument. Actual results could differ materially from those estimates.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(c)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.79pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Restricted Cash&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Restricted cash represents cash that is restricted under the terms of the Company's financing agreement. Such cash is used primarily for construction costs.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(d)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Accounts Receivable and Revenue&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54.7pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Accounts receivable primarily includes receivables from CAISO. The Company generates revenue through transmitting electricity to its customers. The Company's revenue stream consists of FERC approved, tariff-based revenue and is settled by CAISO. The Company recognizes revenue from the transmission of electricity in accordance with ASC 606, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-style: italic"&gt;Revenue from Contracts with Customers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54.7pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54.7pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Performance obligations are satisfied over time as energy is delivered and consumed with billings occurring monthly. Using this output method for revenue recognition provides an accurate depiction of the transfer of electric service as customers obtain control of the commodity and benefit from its use at delivery. Additionally, the Company has an enforceable right to consideration for the delivery of energy at any discrete point in time and will recognize transmission service revenue at an amount that reflects the consideration to which the Company is entitled for the delivery of energy. The Company has elected to apply the practical expedient to recognize revenue in the amount it has the right to invoice, as this represents the value transferred to customers.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54.7pt; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: justify"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company recognizes revenue proportionately throughout the year based on the FERC-approved Transmission Revenue Requirement (TRR) as calculated prior to the beginning of each year and using the FERC-approved formula rate template. CAISO bills load-serving customers for actual usage based on the TRR and estimated load. Differences between the billed rates and the amounts recognized under the TRR are recorded to Transmission Revenue Balancing Accounts (TRBA). With an annual adjustment required by FERC, this ensures that any over-or under-recovery of the annual TRR flow through to transmission customers.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54.7pt; margin-bottom: 0"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Accounts receivable also includes receivables related to construction and operations. At December 31, 2024, this account included $3.3 million related to a refundable credit in relation to payments for land cable from a conductor supply agreement, (see note 5(e)).This amount was fully collected in 2025. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(e)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.79pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Inventory&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Inventory consists of materials and supplies related to transmission work. The Company values inventory at the lower of weighted average cost or net realizable value.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(f)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 16.01pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Property and Equipment&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Property and equipment is stated at cost less accumulated depreciation. Depreciation is computed using the group composite method which is designed to recover the Company's undepreciated property and equipment, adjusted for net salvage, over the remaining life of the assets on a straight- line basis. The estimated life for transmission structures and equipment ranges from 35-45 years, land rights are 60 years, and general equipment and other ranges from 5-20 years. All improvements are capitalized.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(g)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Construction in Progress&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;All costs directly related to the acquisition and construction of long-lived assets are capitalized, including taxes and insurance incurred during the construction. Costs from completed construction projects are transferred to property and equipment when the assets are placed in service or considered used and useful. In 2025, assets constructed under the Gates Project were placed in service and construction in progress for that project was transferred to property and equipment. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, Construction in progress totaled $521.6million and $435.8million, respectively. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In accordance with the FERC Uniform System of Accounts, as provided for in ASC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;980, the Company recognizes allowance for funds used during construction (AFUDC)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;as a cost to construct work in progress. For the years ended December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Company recognized approximately $31.2 million and $17.9 million, respectively in AFUDC on the accompanying statements of operations. The average AFUDC rate utilized in 2025 and 2024 was 8.22% &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;and 7.52%, respectively. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(h)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 13.56pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Impairment of Long-Lived Assets&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In accordance with ASC 360, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-style: italic"&gt;Property, Plant, and Equipment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, long-lived assets with determinable useful lives are reviewed for impairment whenever events or changes in circumstances indicate that the carrying amount of an asset may not be recoverable. Recoverability of assets to be held and used is determined by a specific regulatory disallowance or measured by a comparison of the carrying amount of the assets to the future undiscounted net cash flows expected to be generated by the asset. If such assets are considered to be impaired, the impairment to be recognized is measured by either the amount of regulatory disallowance or the amount by which the carrying amount of the assets exceeds the fair value of the assets and would be charged to earnings.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company currently has approved project sponsor agreements with CAISO. The agreements specify that capital costs for the development, construction and commissioning of the Gates Project and RM Project that will be included in the FERC formula rates, with the exception of excluded costs, are subject to binding capital cost caps. To the extent capital costs for these two projects are lower than the binding capital costs caps, ratepayers will receive a benefit of the lower cost through the approved project sponsor's transmission revenue requirement. In the event capital costs for these two projects are greater than the binding capital cost caps, the Company will not be able to recover the incrementally higher cost in its rates. During 2024, the Company determined that total capital costs for the Gates Project and RM Project would exceed the binding capital cost caps at completion of those projects. In January 2025, the Company exceeded the binding capital cost cap for the Gates Project, and in November 2025, the Company exceeded the binding capital cost cap for the RM Project. The Company determined these capital costs were no longer recoverable. As of December 31, 2025, these costs totaled $5.3 million for the Gates Project and $0.2million for the RM Project and are included as Non-recoverable costs on the accompanying statement of operations.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(i)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 16.62pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Regulatory Assets&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;ASC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;980 provides that the rate actions of a regulator can provide reasonable assurance of the existence of an asset and for the capitalization of costs that would otherwise be charged to expense if it is probable that future revenue will result from inclusion of those capitalized costs in allowable costs for rate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;making purposes. The Company incurred certain costs, including rate filing expenses and other operating costs, which are recoverable through future revenues as a result of including those costs in allowable costs for rate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;making purposes and are deferred in accordance with current regulatory accounting guidance. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Company recognized Regulatory assets on the accompanying balance sheets totaling $4.6 million and $1.7 million, respectively, composed of the items below.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On June 18, 2020, under Docket No. EL20-29, (see note 3), the FERC approved the deferral of recovery of pre-commercial costs incurred to date and going forward that could not be capitalized and would otherwise be expensed for the Gates Project and RM Project.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Pre-commercial expenses deferred include all prudently incurred costs, including, but not limited to, those associated with initial feasibility studies, engineering, consultant and attorney fees, costs associated with seeking regulatory approvals, and carrying costs on pre-commercial costs until included in rate base. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On January 24, 2023, under Docket No. ER23-926, the Company filed a request for specific rate incentives for the Collinsville, Manning, Newark and Metcalf Projects. The incentive rates for these projects include: (1) full recovery of prudently-incurred costs if any of the projects is abandoned for reasons beyond the Company's control and (2) deferred recovery of prudently incurred pre-commercial costs through the creation of a regulatory asset.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;On March 27, 2023, FERC issued an order granting the Company's request for the abandoned plant and regulatory asset incentives for these projects.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;As discussed in Note 2(h), the Company determined that total capital costs for the Gates Project and the RM Project would exceed the binding capital cost caps at completion of those projects. As a result, the Company expensed the full regulatory assets incurred for the Gates Project and the RM Project as required by the respective Approved Project Sponsor Agreements (APSA) and memorialized in attachment 11 of the formula rate template. In 2025 and 2024, the Company expensed $0.5 million and $3.7 million, respectively, and these costs are included as Non-recoverable costs on the accompanying statements of operations. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On March 12, 2025, the Company ended its accrual of costs to the regulatory asset for the Gates, Round Mountain, Collinsville, Manning, Newark, and Metcalf pre-commercial costs, excluding the accrual of carrying charges through August 31, 2025. On July 8, 2025, the Company made its Federal Power Act (FPA) Section 205 filing under Docket No. ER25-2806 seeking FERC approval to commence collection of the regulatory assets over a five year period, and FERC approved the request on August 28, 2025. The regulatory asset, including carrying costs, totaled $2.0 million as of September 6,2025. In accordance with U.S. GAAP, a reserve of $0.1 million was recorded against the regulatory asset for the equity component of the carrying charge, and this will reverse as the recovery occurs through rates. Commencing in September 2025, these costs are being amortized to expense over a five year period. For the year ended December 31, 2025, $127 thousand of regulatory asset amortization was recorded to Operations and maintenance expense and an offsetting $8 thousand reversal of the reserve was recorded to Interest expense, net on the accompanying statement of operations.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The revenues from the Company's cost based formula rates are recorded when earned. The Company achieves matching of costs and related revenues by deferring the net under recovery or over recovery through the TRBA. Any under recovered costs or over recovered revenues are collected from or returned to customers in subsequent periods. For the year ended December 31, 2025, the Company had an under recovery position with a regulatory asset of $2.7 million to be collected in 2027.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Along with the TRBA noted above, the Company has a second TRBA that tracks the under or over recovery of the dollars received from CAISO compared to the revenue calculated in the annual projection. Such TRBA is filed annually with CAISO and collected from or refunded to customers. As of December 31, 2025, the Company is in an under recovery position for this account for the 2025-2026 TRBA cycle and has recorded a regulatory asset of $0.1million, for collection in 2027.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(j)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 16.62pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Asset Retirement Obligation &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In accordance with ASC 410, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-style: italic"&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations and Environmental Obligations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, the Company recognizes the fair value of the liability for an asset retirement obligation (ARO) in the period in which it is incurred if a reasonable estimate of fair value can be made. An amount equal to the present value of the liability is added to the carrying amount of the associated asset and this additional carrying amount is depreciated over the useful life of the asset after the start of commercial operations. The liability is accreted through charges to operating expenses that will be recovered as an allowable cost in future rates.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company has identified, but not recognized, ARO liabilities related to electrical transmission assets, as a result of certain easements on property on which the Company has assets. Generally, such easements are perpetual and require only the retirement and removal of the assets upon cessation of the property's use. Management does not estimate the ARO for such easements because the Company plans to use the facilities indefinitely. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(k)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Debt Issuance and Deferred Financing Costs&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Debt issuance and deferred financing costs are amortized over the term of the Company's financing arrangements using the effective interest method. The amortization of these costs is reflected as a component of Interest expense, net in the accompanying statements of operations. Unamortized debt issuance and deferred financing costs associated with the Company's term loan facilities are reflected as a component of Long term debt on the accompanying balance sheets. Unamortized debt issuance and deferred financing costs associated with revolving loan facilities are reflected as a component of Other assets on the accompanying balance sheets. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(l)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 16.62pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Leases&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In accordance with FASB ASC 842, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-style: italic"&gt;Leases&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, the Company evaluates each contract at inception to determine if it contains a lease. The Company considers a contract to be a lease when an asset is either explicitly or implicitly identified in the contract; and the contract conveys to the Company the right to control the use of the identified asset during the contract period. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Operating lease right of use assets represent our right to use an underlying asset for the lease term, and operating lease liabilities represent our obligation to make lease payments arising from the lease, both of which are recognized based on the present value of the future minimum lease payments over the lease term at the commencement date. Renewal options that are reasonably certain to be exercised are included in the lease term. In determining the present value of the future lease payments, the Company uses the incremental borrowing rate, which is the rate of interest that the Company would have to pay to borrow, on a collateralized basis, over a similar term an amount equal to the payments for the lease. Short-term leases, leases with a term of twelve months or less at inception, are not recorded on the Company's balance sheets. Lease expense for all operating leases is expensed on a straight-line basis over the lease term on the Company's statements of operations.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(m)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 11.12pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Derivative Financial Instruments &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company enters into agreement that meets the definition of a derivative in accordance with ASC 815, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-style: italic"&gt;Derivatives and Hedging&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt; (ASC 815). This agreement is entered into to mitigate or eliminate market and financial risks associated with the financing, construction and operations of the Company.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;ASC 815 provides for three different ways to account for derivative instruments: (i) as an accrual agreement, if the criteria for the &amp;quot;normal purchase normal sale&amp;quot; exception are met and documented; (ii)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;as a cash flow or fair value hedge, if the specified criteria are met and documented; or (iii) as a mark to market agreement with changes in fair value recognized in current period earnings. All derivative instruments that do not qualify for the normal purchase normal sale exception are recorded at fair value in risk management assets and liabilities on the accompanying balance sheets.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;If designated as a cash flow or fair value hedge, the Company documents all relationships between hedging instruments and hedged items, as well as its risk management objective and strategy. This process includes linking all derivatives that are designated as hedges to specific assets or liabilities on the accompanying balance sheets or to forecasted transactions. The Company also assesses, both at the hedge's inception and on an ongoing basis, whether the derivatives that are used in hedging transactions are highly effective in offsetting changes in cash flows of the hedged items. When it is determined that a derivative is not highly effective as a hedge or that it has ceased to be a highly effective hedge, the Company discontinues hedge accounting prospectively. This could occur when: (1) it is determined that a derivative is no longer effective in offsetting changes in the cash flows of a hedged item; (2) the derivative expires or is sold, terminated, or exercised; or (3) the derivative is discontinued as a hedging instrument, because it is unlikely that a forecasted transaction will occur. When hedge accounting is discontinued because it is determined that the derivative no longer qualifies as an effective hedge of cash flows, the derivative will continue to be carried at fair value on the accompanying balance sheets and the gains and losses that were accumulated in other comprehensive income (loss) are recognized immediately or over the remaining term of the forecasted transaction in the accompanying statements of operations.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Changes in the fair value of derivative instruments are either recognized in the accompanying statements of operations or in the accompanying statements of comprehensive income as a component of other comprehensive income or loss, depending upon their use and designation. Gains and losses related to transactions that qualify for hedge accounting are recorded in the accompanying statements of comprehensive income as a component of other comprehensive (loss) income and shown in the aggregate in accumulated other comprehensive income or loss (AOCI) in the accompanying statements of member's equity and will flow through the accompanying statements of operations in the period the hedged item affects earnings. Otherwise, any gains and losses resulting from changes in the market value of the derivative instruments are recorded in the accompanying statements of operations in the current period.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(n)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 13.56pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Regulatory Liabilities&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Under ASC 980, the Company recognizes regulatory liabilities for provisions that have been collected in rates for costs not yet incurred and for amounts that are expected to be refunded to customers in future periods through rates. As of December 31, 2025, the Company recognized Regulatory liabilities on the accompanying balance sheet totaling $0.2 million composed of the items below.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company records cost of removal for its regulated transmission assets through FERC approved depreciation rates and recovers those amounts in rates charged to its customers. The amount recorded as of December 31, 2025is $0.1 million. In 2025, the Company recovered $0.1 million of removal costs through rates and did not incur any removal costs.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Along with the TRBA discussed in Note 2(i), the Company has a second TRBA mechanism, defined in the CAISO tariff, that tracks the under or over recovery of the dollars received from CAISO compared to the revenue requirement calculated through the Company's formula rate during an October through September historic period. This TRBA adjustment is filed annually with FERC and collected from or refunded to customers through an adjustment to revenue requirement in a subsequent rate year. As of December 31, 2025, the Company was in an over recovery TRBA position for this account for the 2024-2025 TRBA adjustment cycle and recorded a regulatory liability of $0.1 million, for collection in 2026. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(o)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Fair Value Measurements&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Fair value, as defined in ASC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;820, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-style: italic"&gt;Fair Value Measurement and Disclosures&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt; (ASC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;820), is the price that would be received to sell an asset or paid to transfer a liability in an orderly transaction between market participants at the measurement date (exit price). The Company utilizes market data or assumptions that market participants would use in pricing the asset or liability, including assumptions about risk and the risks inherent in the inputs to the valuation technique. These inputs can be readily observable, market corroborated, or generally unobservable. The Company primarily applies the market approach for recurring fair value measurements and endeavors to utilize the best available information. Accordingly, the valuation techniques used maximize the use of observable inputs and minimize the use of unobservable inputs.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(p)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Fair Value of Financial Instruments&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The carrying amount of Accounts receivable- trade, Accounts receivable- other, Other current assets, Accounts payable and accrued expenses, Accounts payable - affiliate, Customer deposits, Accrued Retainage and Accrued construction in progress equal or approximate fair value due to the short-term maturity of those instruments. As of December 31, 2025and 2024the fair value of the Company's long-term debt approximated its book value.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(q)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54.7pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company is a limited liability company disregarded for income tax purposes that is indirectly owned (through another disregarded entity) by an entity treated as a corporation for federal income tax purposes. As a result of its disregarded entity status, the Company is not liable for federal or state income taxes. An allocation of the consolidated current and deferred income tax expense of the corporate parent is reported by the Company based on the income tax effects of the Company's activities, including its earnings, gains, losses, deductions, and tax credits for federal and state income tax return reporting. Income taxes are calculated based on the composite federal and state income tax rates applicable to the Company on a stand-alone basis.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54.7pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company is allowed to recover in rates, as a component of its cost of service, an allowance for income taxes and is required to adjust its rate base by the amounts of deferred income tax assets and liabilities that it would have recorded if it were a corporation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;As such, the Company reports deferred taxes on its statement of cash flows and accumulated deferred income taxes and taxes accrued in its balance sheet in this report.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;The Company settles accrued income taxes as a deemed capital contribution from or deemed distribution to its owner. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54.7pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company accounts for income taxes under the asset and liability method, which requires the recognition of deferred tax assets and liabilities for the expected future tax consequences of events that have been included in the financial statements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Under this method, deferred tax assets and liabilities are recognized for temporary differences between the financial statement basis and the tax basis of assets and liabilities using enacted tax rates applicable to for the year(s) in which the temporary differences are expected to reverse.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;In general, deferred income tax expense or benefit for a reporting period is recognized as the change in deferred tax assets and liabilities from the beginning to the end of the period.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54.7pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The effects of tax positions are recognized in the financial statements when it is more likely than not that the position taken, or expected to be taken, in a tax return will be sustained upon examination by taxing authorities based on the technical merits of the position. The effects of changes in tax laws or rates on deferred tax assets and liabilities are recognized in the period of enactment and the timing of recognition of the effects of such changes on income tax expense depends on applicable Commission orders.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54.7pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company recognizes regulatory assets or liabilities for deferred taxes in certain circumstances in accordance with the applicable ratemaking and ASC 980-740. The tax-related regulatory assets recorded relate to the AFUDC and certain carrying charges accrued and capitalized based on an after-tax equity return. Tax-related regulatory assets and liabilities are reported at the revenue requirement level and constitute temporary differences for which deferred tax assets or liabilities must be recorded.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54.7pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Income taxes are discussed in more detail in Note 11, Income Taxes.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(r)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 15.39pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Concentrations of Credit Risk&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Financial instruments, which potentially subject the Company to concentrations of credit risk, consist primarily of Restricted cash and Accounts receivable. Restricted cash is generally held at major financial institutions. Accounts receivable are primarily from CAISO and are concentrated within entities engaged in the energy industry. These industry concentrations may impact the Company's overall exposure to credit risk, either positively or negatively, in that customers may be similarly affected by changes in economic, industry, or other conditions. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(s)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 15.39pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Risks and Uncertainties&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company is subject to a variety of factors, including the economy, regulatory uncertainty as a fully regulated public utility, the possibility of violations of laws, rules, and regulations promulgated by several governmental and quasi-governmental entities, the associated risk of fines and other sanctions, and the availability of capital resources. As with any electric transmission system, operation of the Company's transmission system involves risk, including interruptions or outages due to the breakdown or failure of equipment or processes, violations of permit requirements, operator error, labor disputes, weather interferences or catastrophic events such as fires, earthquakes, floods, explosions, tornadoes, pandemics, or other similar occurrences affecting a transmission system. The occurrence of any of these events could significantly increase expenditures associated with operating and maintaining the transmission system, leaving the Company with less net revenue to make payments of principal and interest on its debt when due.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;As with any major construction effort, construction of the transmission system involves many risks, including shortages of labor, work stoppages, labor disputes, weather interferences, engineering, environmental permitting or geological problems and unanticipated cost increases for reasons beyond the control of the contractor, the occurrence of which could give rise to the delays, performance deficiencies, or otherwise adversely affect the design or operation of the projects.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(t)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 16.62pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Commitments and Contingencies&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In accordance with ASC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;450, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-style: italic"&gt;Contingencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt; (ASC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;450), the Company records a loss contingency for matters when it is probable that a liability has been incurred and the amount of the loss can be reasonably estimated. Loss contingency reserves are based on estimates and judgments made by management with respect to the likely outcome of matters, including any applicable insurance coverage for such matters and are adjusted as circumstances warrant. These estimates and judgments could change based on new information, changes in laws or regulations, changes in management's plans or intentions, the outcome of legal proceedings, settlements or other factors.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;Additionally, the Company follows the guidance of ASC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;460, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-style: italic"&gt;Guarantees&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt; (ASC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;460), for disclosing and accounting of guarantees and indemnifications entered into during the course of business. When a guarantee or indemnification subject to ASC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;460 is entered into the estimated fair value of the guarantee or indemnification is assessed. Some guarantees and indemnifications could have a financial impact under certain circumstances. Management considers the probability of such circumstances occurring when estimating fair value.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 40.18pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -40.18pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;(3)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 27.35pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Regulatory Matters&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(a)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Organizational Filings &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin: 0 7.9pt 0 54.7pt; text-align: justify"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;The Company has received FERC approval to use a company-specific, forward-looking or projected formula rate framework in setting its transmission rates which are collected by CAISO on the Company's behalf. These projected rates are updated annually with forecasted information. Two reconciliations occur annually. The first, a true-up adjustment of the formula rate, calculates the revenue requirement for the prior rate year using actual data. The second, a TRBA adjustment, calculates the difference between revenues due under the effective formula rate and actual revenues received during the trailing October to September period. Both adjustments are incorporated into the projected rates in a subsequent year.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin: 0 7.75pt 0 54.7pt"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On March 4, 2020, under Docket No. EL20-29, the Company filed a petition for a declaratory order seeking FERC authorization for transmission rate incentives for the Gates Project and RM Project pursuant to Section 219 of the FPA. On June 18, 2020, FERC granted the projects:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(1) full recovery of prudently incurred costs if the projects are abandoned for reasons beyond the Company's control; (2) a regulatory asset incentive and accrual of carrying charges; (3) use of a hypothetical capital structure consisting of 55% debt and 45% equity until the projects achieve commercial operation; (4) a 50 basis point return-on-equity risk adder. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On October 23, 2020, under Docket No. ER21-195, the Company filed a transmission formula rate template and formula rate implementation protocols pursuant to FPA Section 205. On June 29, 2021, FERC approved the Company's formula rates and protocols effective December 23, 2020.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;The order accepted certain accumulated deferred income tax (ADIT) worksheets in the formula rate template subject to resolution of a proceeding under Docket No. ER20-1573.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;On March 31, 2022, under Docket No. ER22-1522, the Company filed revised ADIT worksheets following the resolution of Docket No. ER20-1573, which were subsequently accepted by FERC on October 25, 2022.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On January 9, 2023, under Docket No. 23-01-005, the Company filed an application with the CPUC seeking an exemption from the CPUC's affiliate transaction rules and the reporting requirements of certain of the CPUC's general orders.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;This proceeding was settled in March 2024.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On January 10, 2024, under Docket No. ER24-841, PG&amp;amp;E filed a proposed amendment to the interconnection agreement between the Company and PG&amp;amp;E for the electrical interconnection for the Gates Project at PG&amp;amp;E's Gates 500 kV Substation accepted by FERC on December 1, 2022 under FERC Docket No. ER23-34. The revisions were accepted by FERC on March 4, 2024.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On April 29, 2024, under Docket No. 24-04-017, the Company filed an application with the CPUC seeking a Certificate of Public Convenience and Necessity for the Metcalf Project pursuant to CPUC General Order 131-D and authorizations and waivers pursuant to certain other requirements.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On May 14, 2024, under Docket No. ES24-38, the Company filed a request to issue long term debt securities pursuant to FPA Section 204 for the Gates, RM, Collinsville, Manning, Metcalf, and Newark Projects. On July 15, 2024, FERC granted the Company's request.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On May 17, 2024, under Docket No. 24-05-014, the Company filed an application with the CPUC seeking a Certificate of Public Convenience and Necessity for the Newark Project pursuant to CPUC General Order 131-D and authorizations and waivers pursuant to certain other requirements.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On August 1, 2024, as amended on August 16, 2024, under FERC Docket No. ER24-2668, the Company filed revisions to its transmission owner tariff to modify its formula rate template and formula rate protocols to incorporate cost containment measures for Manning, Newark, Metcalf, Collinsville.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;On October 16, 2024, FERC accepted the tariff revisions and granted authorization to continue use of hypothetical capital structure, previously authorized for the Gates Project and RM Project, through completion of the four new projects.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On December 10, 2024, under Docket No. ER25-685, the Company filed revisions to its formula rate implementation protocols to conform to FERC standards.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;FERC accepted the revisions on February 7, 2025.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On June 12, 2025, the Company filed a report notifying FERC that it had entered into an omnibus agreement on May 16, 2025, providing for a term loan and revolving loan facility in the aggregate amount of $650 million. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On July 8, 2025, under Docket No. ER25-2806, the Company submitted a request for authorization to begin amortizing the regulatory assets for pre-commercial costs of Collinsville, Manning, Newark, and Metcalf Projects. On August 28, 2025, FERC issued a delegated letter order accepting the Company's request. &lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;On October 15, 2025, under Docket No. ER26-140 the Company filed revisions to its Transmission Owner Tariff Appendix I, to reflect the annual update of its Transmission Revenue Balancing Account Adjustment (TRBAA) and 2026 Projection which together determine the revenue requirement for the rate year beginning January 1, 2026. On December 19, 2025, FERC issued a letter order accepting the Company's revisions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -27pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;(b)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 14.17pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic"&gt;Formula Rate Updates&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In 2024, the Company projected a 2025 annual revenue requirement of $13.9 million, that included $8.4 million in recoverable operating expenses and no applicable prior year true-up adjustment. The new revenue requirement became effective January 2025, subject to true-up, but no revenues were collected until March 2025 when the Company's initial facilities were placed in service. The calculated 2025 true-up adjustment will be incorporated into the 2027 projected transmission revenue requirement.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 54pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;In 2025, the Company projected a 2026 revenue requirement of $26.3 million, that included $16.5 million in recoverable operating expenses and no applicable prior year true-up adjustment. The new revenue requirement becomes effective January 1, 2026, subject to true-up. The calculated 2026 true-up adjustment will be incorporated into the 2028 projected transmission revenue requirement.&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 40.18pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; text-indent: -40.18pt; page-break-inside: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;(4)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="width: 27.35pt; font: 7pt &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;; display: inline-block"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;Property, equipment and construction in progress, net&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-left: 27.35pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; widows: 2; orphans: 2"&gt; 				&lt;span&gt;As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Property, equipment and construction in progress, net balance is as follows (in thousands):&lt;/span&gt; 			&lt;/p&gt; 			&lt;table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" style="width: 460.95pt; margin-left: 21.95pt; border-collapse: collapse" class="e-rte-table"&gt; 				&lt;tr style="height: 12.95pt"&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.35pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 10pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.45pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 10pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.5pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 10pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.5pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 10pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.45pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 10pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 1.45pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 10pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 230.35pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 10pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 2.7pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 10pt"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 52.2pt; border-bottom: 0.75pt solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1); padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center"&gt; 							&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 2.3pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center"&gt; 							&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 54.35pt; border-bottom: 0.75pt solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 1); padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center"&gt; 							&lt;span style="font-weight: bold"&gt;2024&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 				&lt;/tr&gt; 				&lt;tr style="height: 16.55pt"&gt; 					&lt;td colspan="7" style="width: 303.85pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;Land and land rights&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 2.7pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;$&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 52.2pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; text-align: right"&gt; 							&lt;span&gt;2,370&lt;/span&gt; 						&lt;/p&gt; 					&lt;/td&gt; 					&lt;td style="width: 2.3pt; padding-right: 5.4pt; padding-left: 5.4pt; vertical-align: bottom"&gt; 						&lt;p style="